  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153msrc/[39m (the CommonJS Node side) ever needs to read settings too. Today it doesn't touch the endpoint,
  but the answer fixes the module's export shape, and changing it later means touching every consumer.

  - [1mBrowser-only global script[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m at the repo root, loaded by a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing a single
    [38;5;153mwindow.SETTINGS[39m. Matches exactly how [38;5;153mapp.js[39m already works, zero new tooling. Costs you a global and leaves the Node
    side unable to import it. Cheap to change later while there's one consumer.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153msettings.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m uses [38;5;153mimport[39m. Cleaner and no
    globals, but it converts [38;5;153mapp.js[39m to a module (its [38;5;153mdocument.getElementById[39m wiring still works, but the file now can't
    be opened over [38;5;153mfile://[39m without a local server) and still doesn't interoperate with the CommonJS under [38;5;153msrc/[39m.
  - [1mDual-format (works in both)[0m — one file with a [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m guard so Node and the browser can both load it.
    Genuinely shared, but it's the awkward pattern of the three and only pays off if the Node side actually needs
    settings.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser-only global script.[0m [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is a [38;5;153mgreet()[39m demo with no network concerns; building a
